# Target C-II — the collapsed support-six endpoint is locally realizable and regular

**Date:** 2026-09-18  
**Status:** regression / proved finite local realization; theorem-state authority unchanged  
**Scope:** the degree-pure support-six one-anchor branch with `y_1=...=y_6 != 0`  
**Depends on:** the support-six dichotomy `R=K_6\setminus M`; the one-anchor train-angle closure model; strict unit-contact pentagon separation  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_support6_collapsed_local_realization_2026_09_18.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The support-six reciprocal analysis leaves a collapsed branch in which all six non-ground dual vectors coincide. In that branch all six outside-anchor train directions are projectively parallel, and their oriented unit sides split `3+3`. A natural next attempt was to exclude this branch directly from the six physical pentagon closure equations and the local source-separation inequalities.

That attempt cannot work. There is an exact, certified local realization of the collapsed support-six system satisfying all of those local conditions, and the realization is regular: after two internal train angles are chosen freely, the other ten are smooth functions of them. Thus the collapsed mechanism is not an isolated singular numerical artifact.

This is a regression against a purely local exclusion, not an existence theorem for a global source configuration. The certificate does not construct a globally embedded stopped-train complex or a global minimum-diameter source.

---

## 1. The collapsed octahedral closure system

Relabel the six one-anchor faces by `0,...,5`. Take the missing perfect matching

\[
M=\{03,14,25\},
\]

so the twelve internal relation trains are the edges of

\[
R=K_6\setminus M.
\]

Let the six outside-anchor oriented side vectors be

\[
a_i=s_i e_1,
\qquad
(s_0,s_1,s_2,s_3,s_4,s_5)=(+1,+1,+1,-1,-1,-1).
\tag{1.1}
\]

For each internal edge `ij` with `i<j`, choose an angle `theta_ij` and put

\[
u_{ij}=(\cos\theta_{ij},\sin\theta_{ij}).
\]

The occurrence at face `i` is `u_ij`, while the occurrence at face `j` is `-u_ij`. The six face-closure vectors are therefore

\[
\Phi_i
=
 s_i e_1
 +\sum_{j>i,\ ij\in R}u_{ij}
 -\sum_{j<i,\ ji\in R}u_{ji}.
\tag{1.2}
\]

Every internal train cancels in the global sum, and the anchor signs cancel by (1.1), so

\[
\sum_{i=0}^5\Phi_i=0.
\tag{1.3}
\]

Consequently it is enough to solve the ten scalar equations

\[
\Phi_0=\cdots=\Phi_4=0;
\tag{1.4}
\]

then `Phi_5=0` follows exactly.

---

## 2. Certified regular solution

Fix two internal angles at the exact rational decimals

\[
\theta_{01}=-0.69671336,
\qquad
\theta_{02}=1.75049398.
\tag{2.1}
\]

For the remaining ten angles use the following rational-decimal center `x_0`:

\[
\begin{array}{c|r}
04& 2.731156095605589\\
05&-2.3067260211240153\\
12&-0.9986998192961997\\
13&-2.2337034767798927\\
15& 1.730504059203213\\
23& 1.682595595587784\\
24&-2.1244160181617695\\
34& 2.044414508054986\\
35&-0.7537438854643705\\
45& 2.6878789475216607
\end{array}
\tag{2.2}
\]

Let

\[
F:\mathbb R^{10}\to\mathbb R^{10}
\]

be the scalar closure map in (1.4), with (2.1) fixed, and let `J=DF`. The verifier stores an exact rational matrix `C` obtained by decimal rationalization of a numerical inverse of `J(x_0)`. It then proves all analytic bounds with exact `Fraction` arithmetic. Sine and cosine are enclosed by Taylor polynomials with Lagrange remainders; `pi` is enclosed from Machin's formula.

On the sup-norm box

\[
B=\{x:\|x-x_0\|_\infty\le r\},
\qquad
r=10^{-10},
\]

the exact certificate gives the safe numerical summaries

\[
Y:=\|CF(x_0)\|_\infty<3.503\times10^{-16},
\tag{2.3}
\]

\[
Z_0:=\|I-CJ(x_0)\|_\infty<1.509\times10^{-34},
\qquad
\|C\|_\infty<10.53.
\tag{2.4}
\]

Each scalar closure row contains at most four variable train angles, and every derivative of `sin` or `cos` has magnitude at most one. Hence throughout `B`,

\[
\|J(x)-J(x_0)\|_\infty\le4r.
\]

Therefore

\[
\|I-CJ(x)\|_\infty
\le
Z:=Z_0+4\|C\|_\infty r
<4.212\times10^{-9}<1.
\tag{2.5}
\]

For the Newton-like map

\[
T(x)=x-CF(x),
\]

(2.3)--(2.5) give

\[
\|T(x)-x_0\|_\infty
\le Y+Zr
<3.51\times10^{-16}
<r.
\tag{2.6}
\]

Thus `T` maps `B` strictly into itself and is a contraction there. Banach's fixed-point theorem gives a unique `x_* in B` with `F(x_*)=0`. Equation (1.3) then gives all six face closures.

Moreover `CJ(x_*)` is invertible by (2.5), hence so is the `10x10` derivative with respect to the ten angles in (2.2). The implicit-function theorem therefore makes `theta_01` and `theta_02` genuine local free coordinates:

\[
\boxed{\text{the collapsed local closure solution set has a two-dimensional smooth branch with all six anchor angles fixed.}}
\tag{2.7}
\]

This regularity statement is stronger than the existence of one numerical root.

---

## 3. Every certified face is a strict contact pentagon

For each face, place its anchor vector and four incident internal train vectors in increasing oriented-angle order. The same exact interval certificate, valid on the whole root box, proves

\[
\min\{\text{one-turn gaps}\}>0.4537137,
\tag{3.1}
\]

\[
\max\{\text{one-turn gaps}\}<2.044415
<\frac{2\pi}{3},
\tag{3.2}
\]

and

\[
\max\{\text{two-consecutive-turn sums}\}<3.098316<\pi.
\tag{3.3}
\]

Positive turns summing to `2pi` make each ordered five-vector closure a strict convex equilateral pentagon. For two consecutive unit sides with exterior turn `delta`, the intervening diagonal has length

\[
2\cos(\delta/2).
\]

By (3.2) this is strictly greater than one. Every diagonal of a pentagon is of this form, so no extra unit chord is created. Equation (3.3) also rules out any projectively parallel side pair.

Thus the certified block satisfies the same local source-derived conditions used throughout the support-five/support-six reciprocal analysis:

- exact unit-vector face closure;
- strict convexity;
- no unit contact chord inside a face;
- no projectively parallel side pair;
- every exterior turn strictly below `2pi/3`;
- every two consecutive exterior turns summing to strictly less than `pi`.

The cyclic side orders are robust on the whole certified box.

---

## 4. The natural side pairing is topologically consistent

Pair the two occurrences of each of the twelve internal train sides by translation and leave the six anchor sides unpaired. Using the certified cyclic side orders, the quotient has

\[
V=8,
\qquad E=18,
\qquad F=6,
\]

and exactly two boundary components. They contain the anchor faces

\[
\{0,2,5\},
\qquad
\{1,3,4\}.
\]

Hence

\[
\chi=8-18+6=-4
=2-2g-2,
\]

so

\[
\boxed{g=2.}
\tag{4.1}
\]

This is an auxiliary force/side-pairing surface, not the physical planar source. Its role here is negative: even the obvious translation-gluing consistency does not produce an Euler obstruction to the collapsed block.

---

## 5. What this rules out and what remains

The collapsed support-six branch cannot be eliminated using only the abstract octahedral relation graph, six parallel anchors with a `3+3` orientation split, the six local unit-pentagon closures, strict source separation, or the existing local turn-gap/no-parallel-side lemmas. Those conditions admit a robust exact solution.

This does **not** prove that a collapsed support-six component occurs in a canonical source. The certificate does not establish:

- a global planar embedding of all stopped train corridors and rhombi;
- compatibility with the actual activated/outer complex beyond keeping the six anchor train angles fixed;
- a source-point embedding realizing the entire contact graph;
- compatibility with the actual diameter bars and KKT locking system;
- global minimum-diameter optimality.

Accordingly the support-six frontier splits cleanly:

1. the noncollapsed branch is still the sixteen finite reciprocal `H5` metric cases (six uniform and ten simple-wall orbits);
2. the collapsed branch is locally feasible and must be attacked with a genuinely global train/source constraint, or bypassed by a flex-versus-diameter-locking argument.

No theorem-state file is changed.

**Disposition:** certified a regular exact local realization of the collapsed support-six endpoint, with a two-parameter anchor-fixed closure family and all strict local contact-pentagon inequalities. This rejects a purely local collapsed-branch exclusion. C-II remains open.
